\name{shift0}
\alias{shift0}
\title{Zero-Filled Lag and Lead Operator}
\description{
Shifts a vector forward or backward by a specified number of positions and
fills out-of-range values with zeros. This helper is designed for recursive and
bootstrap constructions where missing values should be treated as zero rather
than \code{NA}, closely mirroring zero-filled lag/lead operations used in
simulation-based algorithms.
}
\usage{
shift0(v, k)
}
\arguments{
  \item{v}{A numeric vector.}

  \item{k}{Integer. Shift order. Positive values correspond to lags
  (shifting the series downward, i.e.\ \eqn{t-k}), while negative values
  correspond to leads (shifting the series upward, i.e.\ \eqn{t+k}).}
}
\details{
This function performs a position-based shift of the input vector \code{v}.
Unlike strict time-indexed helpers (such as \code{\link{get_lag}}), \code{shift0()}
does not rely on an explicit time index and does not propagate gaps. Instead, it
is intended for internal simulation steps where the data are already aligned
and missing values should be interpreted as zeros.

Let \eqn{n} denote the length of \code{v}. The behaviour is:
\itemize{
  \item \code{k = 0}: return \code{v} unchanged,
  \item \code{k > 0}: lag by \code{k} positions; the first \code{k} elements are zero,
  \item \code{k < 0}: lead by \code{|k|} positions; the last \code{|k|} elements are zero.
}

Formally, for \code{k > 0},
\deqn{
\text{out}_t =
\begin{cases}
0, & t \le k, \\
v_{t-k}, & t > k,
\end{cases}
}
and for \code{k < 0},
\deqn{
\text{out}_t =
\begin{cases}
v_{t+|k|}, & t \le n-|k|, \\
0, & t > n-|k|.
\end{cases}
}
}
\value{
A numeric vector of the same length as \code{v}, containing the shifted values
with zeros inserted where the shift would otherwise go out of range.
}
\section{Usage in Simulations}{
This section explains when and why \code{shift0()} is useful, particularly in
bootstrap and simulation contexts.

\subsection{Why zero-filling?}{
In many recursive data-generating processes, missing lagged or lead values are
interpreted as zero by construction (e.g., initial conditions in AR processes or
when simulating innovations). \code{shift0()} provides a simple and explicit
zero-filled alternative to avoid \code{NA} propagation.
}

\subsection{Difference from get_lag()}{
Unlike \code{\link{get_lag}}, \code{shift0()} is position-based and ignores
time indices. Use \code{shift0()} when simulating aligned series where
zeros are the appropriate boundary condition.
}
}
\examples{
v <- c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)

## Lag by one (k = 1)
shift0(v, 1)
# [1] 0 1 2 3 4

## Lead by one (k = -1)
shift0(v, -1)
# [1] 2 3 4 5 0

## Larger shifts
shift0(v, 2)
# [1] 0 0 1 2 3

shift0(v, -2)
# [1] 3 4 5 0 0
}
